## 1.  What is a Thread?

A **thread** is the smallest unit of execution within a Java program.  
All Java programs run as a single process, and that process can contain one or more threads that execute *concurrently*.  
The JVM schedules these threads for you – you just need to *create* them and *start* them.

### Thread life‑cycle (simplified)

| State      | Meaning                                                                 |
|------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **New**    | `Thread` object has been created but `start()` hasn’t been called yet.  |
| **Runnable** | `start()` has been called; the thread is eligible to run.               |
| **Running** | The thread is currently executing.                                      |
| **Blocked** | Waiting for a monitor lock (e.g., inside a `synchronized` block).      |
| **Waiting** | `wait()`, `join()`, `sleep()` etc. – the thread is paused.              |
| **Timed Waiting** | Same as Waiting, but for a limited time (e.g., `sleep(1000)`).       |
| **Terminated** | The thread’s `run()` method has finished.                           |

> **Key point:**  
> • `Thread.start()` **creates a new thread** and calls its `run()` method in that new thread.  
> • `Thread.run()` **does NOT start a new thread** – it just executes the code *in the current thread*.

---

## 2.  Two ways to create a thread

| Approach | When to use | Typical code |
|----------|-------------|--------------|
| **Extend `Thread`** | When you control the thread class itself | `class MyThread extends Thread { … }` |
| **Implement `Runnable`** | Preferred – separates *what* to do from *how* to run it | `class MyTask implements Runnable { … }` |

The `Runnable` approach is more flexible because you can pass the same `Runnable` to different `Thread` objects, or to an `ExecutorService`.

---

## 3.  A very simple example

We’ll write a program that creates two threads, each printing a sequence of numbers.  
The two sequences will intermix because the threads run concurrently.

```java
// 1) The work that will run in a thread
class CounterTask implements Runnable {
    private final String name;   // name of the thread (for clarity)
    private final int start;     // first number to print
    private final int end;       // last number to print

    CounterTask(String name, int start, int end) {
        this.name = name;
        this.start = start;
        this.end = end;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        // run() is executed in the new thread
        for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
            System.out.println(name + ": " + i);
            // give the scheduler a chance to switch threads
            try { Thread.sleep(50); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
        }
    }
}

// 2) The main program
public class SimpleMultithreadedDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create two runnable tasks
        Runnable task1 = new CounterTask("Thread-1", 1, 5);
        Runnable task2 = new CounterTask("Thread-2", 6, 10);

        // Create two Thread objects and start them
        Thread t1 = new Thread(task1);
        Thread t2 = new Thread(task2);

        t1.start();   // <--- thread t1 begins running
        t2.start();   // <--- thread t2 begins running

        // Optional: wait for both threads to finish
        try {
            t1.join();
            t2.join();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }

        System.out.println("Both threads have finished.");
    }
}
```

### What you’ll see

```
Thread-1: 1
Thread-2: 6
Thread-1: 2
Thread-2: 7
Thread-1: 3
Thread-2: 8
Thread-1: 4
Thread-2: 9
Thread-1: 5
Thread-2: 10
Both threads have finished.
```

The exact order can vary because the scheduler decides when to switch threads.

